PLACES=[
N('historic-salem-courthouse','Historic Salem Courthouse (1869 Washington County courthouse)','Salem','wash','civic','standing',43.17298,-73.32148,'exact','public','drive','','1869',
  'Washington County\'s courthouse from 1869, designed by the Troy architect Marcus Cummings and built for $30,000 to replace the wooden courthouse of 1796 on Main Street. The old Main Street jail was moved next to it; a new jail designed by William J. Beardsley, architect of Attica prison, was finished in 1906. The town of Salem owns it, and a non-profit runs it as a community centre. It is the eastern end of the Salem Historic District.',
  'Decorative brickwork on the front, round arches, semicircular stained-glass windows and broad stairways, and inside the original 1869 courtroom, which a preservation architect quoted by Lakes to Locks Passage calls one of the best preserved 19th-century courtrooms in the state. Wikipedia says the building is as built apart from its lost bell tower. The attached 1906 jail stands beside it. Tours are offered (Lakes to Locks Passage page).',
  'A county courtroom of 1869 that nobody modernised.',
  'Owned by the Town of Salem and run by the Historic Salem Courthouse Preservation Association, 58 East Broadway (NY 153). Open Monday to Friday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m., weekends on request or for events; no fee; tours offered (Lakes to Locks Passage page, read November 2026; the association\'s own site was not opened). Parking not checked: the nearest mapped lots are on Warren and McDougal streets, 240 to 300 m north.',
  [SALEMCH,POMCH,SALEMTOUR,W('Salem Historic District (New York)')],
  brick='?'),
N('salem-revolutionary-war-cemetery','Revolutionary War Cemetery and stockade fort hill, Salem','Salem','wash','burial','standing',43.16722,-73.32972,'site','public','walk','cemeteries,forts','1769',
  'Salem\'s first burying ground, laid out by the Pelham, Massachusetts settlers of 1764 and the Scotch-Irish congregation that came from Ballybay in 1766; the earliest recorded burial is 1769, and it was the only public burying ground until Evergreen Cemetery opened in 1859. The town counts 105 Revolutionary War soldiers and five of the War of 1812 here; the village\'s 1962 marker says more Revolutionary soldiers lie here than in any other cemetery in the county. A county historical society marker on NY 22 north of Park Place, about 350 m north of the gate, says a stockaded fort was built atop that hill in 1778 and held by Charlotte County militia; whether that is the cemetery hill was not confirmed. On the National Register since 2004.',
  'An old hillside burying ground at Archibald Street and NY 22 (South Main Street), with several markers and monuments by the gate; the graves were mapped and indexed in 2000 and the town is restoring the stones. The stockade fort is known only from its marker; nothing of it is described as surviving.',
  '',
  'Town of Salem burying ground (the town\'s cemetery committee looks after it). Entrance at Archibald Street and NY 22. Hours not checked: visit in daylight and keep off the stones. No lot is mapped nearby; park on the street in the village centre.',
  [SALEMCEM,SALEMTOUR,HM(58530,'Revolutionary Cemetery'),HM(129208,'Stockaded Fort')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('salem-historic-district','Salem village: Main Street and Broadway','Salem','wash','town','standing',43.17194,-73.3275,'area','view','walk','','1790',
  'Salem was settled about 1764 by families from Pelham, Massachusetts, joined in 1766 by Thomas Clark\'s Scotch-Irish congregation, and was incorporated as a village in 1803 (it was dissolved into the town in 2016). Fort Salem, built round the unfinished 1774 New England Presbyterian church, was burned by Loyalists in 1777. A fire in 1840 took much of the centre; what stood through it and what was built after makes up the 79-building historic district along Main Street (NY 22) and Broadway, listed in 1975.',
  'Walk the cross of Main Street and Broadway. The town\'s landmark tour names: the 1836 brick New England Presbyterian church, now the Fort Salem Theater, on the site of the Revolutionary fort (11 East Broadway); the late-Georgian Blanchard house with its Palladian window, dated 1790 by Wikipedia and 1796 by the town (19 East Broadway); St. Paul\'s Episcopal of 1860 with an E. & G. Hook tracker organ and Tiffany glass (32 East Broadway); the First United Presbyterian church of 1797 (13 West Broadway); the Proudfit Building of 1890, village hall, library and theatre; the red brick Union Engine House of 1866; and the big brick Manhattan Shirt Company factory of 1899 at Park Place, now being restored. Interiors not checked apart from what the tour says.',
  'A Revolutionary frontier village rebuilt after the fire of 1840.',
  'Public streets; the houses and churches are private and seen from the sidewalk. The courthouse at the east end is the one building with set hours (its own entry). Parking: street parking, and mapped lots on Warren and McDougal streets.',
  [SALEMTOUR,W('Salem Historic District (New York)'),HM(58527,'The White Creek Fort'),HM(58523,'Old White Church')],
  conf='mixed',brick='some'),
N('asa-fitch-house-salem','Martin–Fitch House and Asa Fitch\'s "Bug House" laboratory','Salem','wash','landmark','standing',43.15015,-73.37967,'area','view','drive','','c. 1787',
  'Home of Asa Fitch (1809-1879), the first state entomologist (1854-71) and, by the National Register\'s account, the first occupational entomologist in the United States, a pioneer of the study of crop pests. The house was built about 1787 and altered about 1796-1812 and again about 1830. His small frame laboratory, the "Bug House", was built about 1825 and enlarged about 1860. Listed on the National Register in 2014.',
  'A two-story, five-bay late-Georgian timber-frame house with a steep hipped slate roof, dormers and two inside chimneys; beside it the small two-story gabled laboratory with a lean-to, an old barn and a milk house of about 1900. A state marker stands on NY 29 west of Salem village.',
  'A frontier farmhouse with America\'s first bug lab in the yard.',
  'Private home; see it from NY 29 only and do not go up any drive. The pin is the roadside marker, not the house. No parking: stop only where the shoulder is safe. Fitch is buried in Evergreen Cemetery in Salem (town landmark tour).',
  [W('Martin–Fitch House and Asa Fitch Jr. Laboratory'),HM(62954,'Home and Laboratory of Dr. Asa Fitch, Jr.'),SALEMTOUR],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('hubbard-hall-cambridge','Hubbard Hall opera house','Cambridge','wash','amuse','standing',43.02815,-73.38001,'exact','public','drive','','1878',
  'A rural opera house opened in November 1878 by the Cambridge businessman Martin Hubbard, who built it to outdo Ackley Hall on the village\'s rival west side. Until 1927 it put on travelling operas, plays, concerts, magic acts and lectures, and served for rallies, graduations and church services; Susan B. Anthony led a two-day suffrage convention here in 1894. Then the hall upstairs stood dark for fifty years while the shops below stayed open. A community group bought it for about $25,000 in 1977 and runs it as an arts centre.',
  'An 1878 hall that Hubbard Hall says was designed by the architect of the Troy Music Hall; the Wikipedia picture shows a stucco and half-timbered front with brown peaked roofs. Upstairs the hall has its 19th-century stage; behind it the old rail freight yard buildings were restored in 2006 (Beacon Feeds studio, Freight Depot theatre, Lovejoy building). How much of the gas-lit era decoration survives inside was not checked.',
  'Susan B. Anthony spoke here; then the lights went off for fifty years.',
  'Owned and run by Hubbard Hall Projects, a non-profit, 25 East Main Street (NY 372) at Washington Street. The ground floor holds a gallery, a craft market and a coffee shop; the hall is open for shows (box office Tuesday, Thursday and Friday, 11 a.m. to 3 p.m., and 30 minutes before a show) (Hubbard Hall pages, read November 2026). Parking in the Hubbard Hall and village lots off Washington Street, behind the hall, and on the street.',
  [HHVISIT,HHHIST,HM(160004,'Hubbard Hall'),W('Cambridge Historic District (Cambridge, New York)')],
  brick='no'),
N('cambridge-historic-district','Cambridge village: Main Street, St. Luke\'s and the Rice Seed Company','Cambridge','wash','town','standing',43.0275,-73.3828,'area','view','walk','','1779',
  'Three hamlets, Cambridge, Dorr Corners and North White Creek, grew round crossroads on the Cambridge Patent of 1761 and merged as the village of Cambridge in 1866, which is why its streets meet at odd angles. The railroad came in 1852; the Rice Seed Company, by 1900 the largest seed company in the world with 200 local workers, made the village its headquarters from 1879 until it closed in 1976. The 240-building historic district was listed in 1978.',
  'From Wikipedia\'s summary of the Register form: the Dorr-Randall House of 1779 at 151 East Main, the oldest in the village, with fine carved Federal ornament; St. Luke\'s church of 1866 at 4 St. Luke\'s Place, with a Tiffany interior and windows; the Rice Seed warehouse of 1879 and office of 1895 at 15 West Main, with Jerome Rice\'s house across the street; Hubbard Hall (its own entry); the 1838/1861 Methodist church at 47 East Main and the 1844 Baptist church at 3 West Main. Across East Main from the Old White Church is a cemetery where a DAR marker says twenty Revolutionary soldiers lie. Whether St. Luke\'s is open to look inside was not checked.',
  'The village that sold seeds to the world.',
  'Public streets; the churches and houses are private or in use and seen from the street. Parking on the street and in the village lots off Washington Street.',
  [W('Cambridge Historic District (Cambridge, New York)'),HM(129214,'Old White Church Cemetery')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('white-creek-historic-district','White Creek hamlet','White Creek','wash','town','standing',42.97167,-73.28944,'area','view','drive','','1770',
  'A crossroads hamlet of twenty historic buildings put up between 1770 and 1885, mostly Greek Revival with Federal and Queen Anne ones among them, listed on the National Register in 1979. A town marker on County Route 68 gives it the site of Union Academy, founded in 1810 and called there the first free academy in New York State.',
  'Mostly houses, with Greek Revival commercial buildings: a church, a hotel or tavern, a general store and a harness shop (Wikipedia). Wikipedia\'s pictures name the Christopher Allen House, the John Allen Jr. House, the Methodist (Baptist) church and a marker for John Allen\'s hat shop. The academy is gone; only its marker is there. Interiors not checked.',
  '',
  'Private houses along County Route 68 and its cross roads; see them from the road. No parking area was found: stop only where the shoulder is safe.',
  [W('White Creek Historic District'),HM(193189,'Union Academy')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('hartford-baptist-church','Hartford Baptist Church, cemetery and old church footings','Hartford','wash','civic','standing',43.36485,-73.39229,'exact','view','drive','cemeteries','1891',
  'The congregation gathered by 1787 and met in a log barn; in 1798 it was given land by DeWitt Clinton, raised a log meeting house in 1805 (by the church\'s marker; Wikipedia dates the first church on the site to 1789) and in 1813 a 51 by 55 foot meeting house that seated 700. That burned on May 4, 1890, and the present church, designed by the Philadelphia architect Benjamin Price, was dedicated on January 22, 1891, the fourth on the site. Wikipedia says the members passed anti-Masonic resolutions, recruited soldiers for the Union out of abolitionism, and lost their third church to a fire laid over their temperance stand. On the National Register since 2004.',
  'A Victorian Gothic brick church with a tall wooden bell tower, the only one of its kind in the town, on a 4.4-acre lot; the cemetery south and east of it holds early settlers and Revolutionary veterans. North and west of the church are the remaining footings of the earlier, larger church. Interior not checked.',
  'Four churches on one lot, and the footings of the third still in the grass.',
  'An active church on Main Street (County Route 23) at the north end of the hamlet; the grounds are church property. See the church and footings from the road or the cemetery in daylight; hours not checked. Parking not checked; no lot is mapped.',
  [W('Hartford Baptist Church'),HM(298000,'First Baptist Church / Elder Amasa Brown')],
  conf='mixed',brick='yes'),
N('hartford-museum-enlistment-center','Hartford Museum (Elisha Straight House) and Civil War Enlistment Center','Hartford','wash','civic','standing',43.36481,-73.39326,'site','public','drive','','early 1800s',
  'Two buildings in the centre of the hamlet, both on the National Register. The museum is in the Elisha Straight house of the early 1800s, which the town calls the oldest remaining house in the hamlet. In the second building 65 of Hartford\'s 134 Civil War soldiers enlisted in Company E of the 123rd New York Volunteers on August 22, 1862 (county marker); the town page calls it the only Civil War enlistment centre left in New York State.',
  'A red wooden house holding furniture, books and things from 19th-century Hartford, and the enlistment building with the company\'s equipment and story. About 300 m south on NY 40 by the fire house, a Hartford Historical Group marker lists the mills that stood on the creek: I. Norton\'s dam of 1814, Downs clothing works, an 1866 starch mill, an 1870 cabinet works and an 1818 tannery; whether anything of them is left was not checked.',
  '',
  'Run by the Hartford Historical Group, a non-profit. Open Saturdays 1 to 4 p.m. in July and August and by appointment; free (Town of Hartford page, read November 2026). The marker is at County Route 23 and East Street in front of the museum. Parking not checked; no lot is mapped.',
  [HARTMUS,W('Elisha Straight House'),HM(63515,'Civil War Enlistment Center'),HM(61515,'Site of Old Mills')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('browns-tavern-south-hartford','Brown\'s Tavern','South Hartford','wash','hotel','standing',43.34472,-73.40972,'site','view','drive','','1802',
  'A tavern built in 1802 by Caleb Brown (state marker of 1935) at the crossroads of NY 40 and NY 196, and the farm that grew round it. The tavern was redone about 1878-80 in a regional eclectic style; the farm has an 1878 horse barn. Listed on the National Register in 2000.',
  'A tall-gabled tavern 47 by 37 feet, five bays by three, with Federal details under the later trim; inside, a noted ballroom (Wikipedia; not seen). Round it: the 1878 horse barn, an outhouse, a corn crib, a well and pump, and the foundations of the store and sheds.',
  'A turnpike tavern with its ballroom still upstairs.',
  'Private property; see it from NY 40 or NY 196 and do not go in the yard. No parking: stop only where the shoulder is safe.',
  [W('Brown\'s Tavern'),HM(61514,'Brown\'s Tavern')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('south-granville-congregational-church','South Granville Congregational Church','South Granville','wash','civic','standing',43.37194,-73.28694,'exact','view','drive','','1840s',
  'One of the first churches in the town, founded when settlement began after the Revolution; a 1789 marker on the green calls it the first Congregational church in Granville. Lemuel Haynes, the first African-American ordained minister in North America, was one of its pastors. The present frame church, the fourth, dates from the 1840s and was enlarged in 1873 with Gothic touches; a Greek Revival parsonage stands beside it. Both were listed on the National Register in 2005.',
  'A white frame church on a stone foundation, now vinyl over clapboard, with a two-stage bell tower and mansard top and carved scrolls over the round-arched front windows. Inside (Wikipedia): oak moulding round the double doors and twelve rows of oak pews down the nave. The parsonage is south of it on the semicircular drive, with a state marker on the green. Whether the church opens to visitors was not checked.',
  '',
  'On the north side of NY 149 at the west edge of South Granville; church property. Seen from the drive and road; hours not checked. Parking not checked.',
  [W('South Granville Congregational Church'),HM(131725,'Erected 1789')],
  brick='no'),
N('lemuel-haynes-house','Lemuel Haynes House','South Granville','wash','landmark','standing',43.37126,-73.28288,'site','view','drive','','1793',
  'Lemuel Haynes (1753-1833), born to a white mother and a father of African descent, grew up indentured, served in the militia in the Revolutionary War and became the first African-American clergyman ordained in North America. This house of 1793 was his home from 1822 until his death. It was made a National Historic Landmark in 1975 and is a private home, not normally open.',
  'A one-and-a-half-story clapboarded frame house with a central chimney, its five-bay front facing east, side-on to County Route 27. Wikipedia says it keeps wide floorboards and a big kitchen fireplace with a pot crane and beehive oven (inside, not to be seen).',
  'Home of the first ordained Black minister in America.',
  'Private residence on the south side of County Route 27 just east of NY 149; see it from the road only. The church he served is 300 m west (its own entry).',
  [W('Lemuel Haynes House')],
  brick='no'),
N('slate-valley-museum','Slate Valley Museum','Granville','wash','industry','standing',43.40599,-73.26182,'exact','public','drive','','c. 1840',
  'The museum of slate quarrying in the slate valley of New York and Vermont, the only place that produces red slate as well as green, purple, grey and black. It opened in 1995 in a New World Dutch barn of about 1840, perhaps with parts from the 1700s, which stood in Ravena and was given by the Blue Circle Cement Company, moved here and enlarged. It won a county preservation award for the barn in 1996.',
  'Inside the H-framed Dutch barn: a quarry shanty with the machinery and tools of hand quarrying, displays on the geology and working of slate, and a WPA mural; the streets round it once held quarry workers\' row houses (county tourism page).',
  '',
  'Run by the non-profit Slate Valley Museum, 17 Water Street. 2027 season May 22 to October 16: Tuesday to Friday noon to 4 p.m., Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; closed July 4; a holiday festival in early December; groups and research by appointment year round. Adults $7, seniors and students $5, family $20, children 12 and under free (museum page, read November 2026). Parking: the Water Street lot beside the museum.',
  [SVM,SVMLTL],
  th=[43.40571,-73.26144],thn='Water Street lot',brick='no'),
N('granville-town-hall-post-office','Granville town hall (Farmer\'s National Bank and Hughes Slate office) and post office','Granville','wash','civic','standing',43.4075,-73.26278,'exact','view','walk','','1891',
  'Two Register buildings on Main Street (NY 149). The town hall was built in 1891 for the Farmer\'s National Bank and the W. H. Hughes Slate Company, enlarged twice between about 1912 and 1943, and has held the town offices since about 1945. Along the street the Colonial Revival post office of the mid-1930s, by the Treasury\'s Louis Simon, is one of three in the state with a raised parapet on its roof line and has an unusual lopsided front.',
  'The town hall is two stories of cut limestone and brick with a Romanesque arched entrance. The post office is brick, five by six bays, on a raised foundation faced in slate at the front. Interiors not checked.',
  '',
  'Town offices and a working post office; see them from the street, and go in during office hours (not checked). Street parking, and the post office lot behind it reached from the west drive.',
  [W('Farmer\'s National Bank and W.H. Hughes Slate Company Office'),W('United States Post Office (Granville, New York)')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('dayton-williams-house-middle-granville','Dayton-Williams House and Middle Granville','Middle Granville','wash','landmark','standing',43.43148,-73.28091,'area','view','drive','','c. 1820',
  'A Federal brick house of about 1820 in Middle Granville, which a county marker says was founded in 1778 as a business centre and where William R. Williams opened the first slate quarry in 1853, bringing Welsh settlers. Listed on the National Register in 2006.',
  'A two-story, five-bay, centre-entrance brick house, gable end to the road. On the lot are a retaining wall of fieldstone and slate and the foundation of a 19th-century house built for railroad workers (Wikipedia). A small pioneer cemetery and the county marker are on Main Street (County Route 24).',
  '',
  'Private home; see it from the road. The pin is the hamlet marker on Main Street, not the house, which was not found on the aerial. Street parking in the hamlet not checked.',
  [W('Dayton-Williams House'),HM(137918,'Middle Granville')],
  conf='mixed',brick='yes'),
N('easton-friends-north-meetinghouse','Easton Friends North Meetinghouse','North Easton','wash','civic','standing',43.05695,-73.53417,'exact','view','drive','','1838',
  'Quakers from Dartmouth and Nantucket settled Easton from about 1775. This second meeting house, on what is now NY 40, was built in 1838; the first, of 1787, still stands on Meeting House Road. The meeting helped fugitive slaves on the Underground Railroad; Lucretia Mott and Sojourner Truth spoke there, and Susan B. Anthony was a member for some years (Easton Library). Listed on the National Register in 2005.',
  'A one-story rectangular brick meeting house with a gable roof (Wikipedia); the Wikipedia photograph shows red-brown brick walls on a stone base. Interior not checked.',
  'The Quaker meeting where Susan B. Anthony sat.',
  'Still used by Easton Friends Meeting, 1778 State Route 40: worship Sundays at 11 a.m. here from mid-September to June (summer meetings are at the South Meetinghouse) (New York Yearly Meeting page, read November 2026). Otherwise see it from the road. No lot is mapped.',
  [NYYM,EASTLIB,W('Easton Friends North Meetinghouse')],
  brick='yes'),
N('fort-miller-reformed-church','Fort Miller Reformed Church','Fort Miller','wash','civic','standing',43.16361,-73.58194,'site','view','drive','','1816',
  'A Federal-style church of 1816 on Fort Miller Road, west of US 4 and south of Galusha Island, built as a two-story clapboard sanctuary with a four-story bell tower and joined to a carriage and horse shed of about 1818-22; a rear wing was added in 1896. The complex also holds a parsonage and a custodian\'s house, both of about 1845, and a late-19th-century barn. Listed on the National Register in 1996.',
  'The clapboard church and tower, the long horse shed attached to it (rare to survive), the two 1840s houses and the barn. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Church property on Fort Miller Road; whether services are still held was not checked. See it from the road. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Fort Miller Reformed Church Complex')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('hebron-valley-grange','Hebron Valley Grange (West Hebron Methodist meeting house)','West Hebron','wash','civic','standing',43.23056,-73.37611,'site','view','drive','','c. 1839',
  'Built about 1839 as a Methodist meeting house on a site Wikipedia puts 20 miles away, and moved twice, the second time before 1874. The local Grange bought it in 1911 and used it until December 1985. Listed on the National Register in 2006.',
  'A three-by-three-bay, one-story heavy timber-frame building with an addition of about 1874. Present use and interior not checked.',
  '',
  'In the hamlet of West Hebron; owner and use not checked. See it from the road.',
  [W('Hebron Valley Grange No. 1103')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('whitehall-main-street-historic-district','Whitehall Main Street','Whitehall','wash','town','standing',43.55557,-73.4021,'site','view','walk','canal','1865',
  'After the fire of 1864, Whitehall rebuilt its business street facing the Champlain Canal: a three-block row of two- and three-story brick and stone commercial buildings put up between 1865 and 1900, most designed by the local architect Almon Chandler Hopson, who also designed Skene Manor on the hill above. Forty buildings make up the district, listed in 1975.',
  'The canal-front row, with a former Montgomery Ward store among it (Wikipedia picture of 2009), and the Pomeroy marker on Main Street north of Saunders Street. The canal walls and the Skenesborough Museum are a short walk south (other entries). Interiors not checked.',
  '',
  'Public street; shops and offices in use. Parking: street parking; OpenStreetMap maps small lots on Main Street by the marker and off Skenesborough Drive, which were not checked on the aerial.',
  [W('Main Street Historic District (Whitehall, New York)'),HM(249394,'Main Street Historic District')],
  brick='yes'),
N('whitehall-armory','Whitehall Armory','Whitehall','wash','civic','standing',43.54978,-73.39619,'exact','view','drive','','1899',
  'A castle-like National Guard armory of 1899 by the state architect Isaac G. Perry, at 62 Poultney Street. The 9th Separate Company and its successors drilled here; the Guard left in 2009, and the state military museum says the building was sold to a private developer in 2011 and became the Whitehall Athletic Club in 2012. Listed on the National Register in 1995.',
  'Brick and stone: a two-story hip-roofed administration block with a five-story octagonal tower and a shorter round tower, and a gable-roofed drill shed behind. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Privately owned; whether the club admits visitors was not checked. See it from Poultney or Williams Street. Street parking; a mapped lot on Williams Street.',
  [DMNA,W('Whitehall Armory')],
  conf='mixed',brick='yes'),
N('greenwich-village-historic-district','Greenwich village (Union Village) and its Underground Railroad houses','Greenwich','wash','town','standing',43.09167,-73.49861,'area','view','walk','','1840',
  'A 165-building historic district, listed in 1995, covering the centre of the village: houses, shops, churches and civic buildings mostly of 1840-1900, and six memorial parks, among them Monument Park with its 1916 Civil War monument. The village was called Union Village from 1809 to 1867 and was a centre of the anti-slavery movement; a marker names houses of abolitionists who sheltered fugitives on the route from Troy through Easton.',
  'Walk Main and Church streets. The marker lists 18 Church Street (Henry Holmes), 6 Church Street (William and Angelina Mowry), 146 and 150 Main Street (Edwin Masters, Leonard Gibbs), the Free Congregational Church parsonage at 28 Main Street and 2 Academy Street (Edwin Andrews). The Free Congregational Church itself, where Frederick Douglass and William Lloyd Garrison spoke, later became an opera house and burned in 1913. Interiors not checked.',
  '',
  'Public streets; the houses are private and seen from the sidewalk. Street parking in the village; lots not checked.',
  [W('Village of Greenwich Historic District'),HM(172612,'Underground Railroad Stops in Union Village')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
]
